 \subsection{Synthetic Data Generation for Tabular Data}
-Tabular SDG methods fall into four families, which differ in how they produce new records. \emph{Interpolation and statistical methods} derive new records directly from the real ones or from a simple probabilistic model of them: SMOTE places a new minority-class record on the line segment between two neighbouring records of that class~\cite{chawla2002smote}, and Bayesian networks estimate the dependencies between features and sample new records from them~\cite{zhang2014privbayes}. \emph{Deep generative models} train a neural network to approximate the joint distribution of the features and then sample from it: CTGAN trains a generator against a discriminator, TVAE learns a compressed representation of the records~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, and diffusion models such as TabDDPM, CoDi and TabSyn learn to reverse a gradual corruption of the records~\cite{kotelnikov2023tabddpm,lee2023codi,zhang2024tabsyn}; relational and cross-silo variants extend these models to several tables or institutions~\cite{fan2020relational,shankar2024silofuse}. \emph{Fine-tuned language models} write each record as a sentence, fine-tune a language model on these sentences and generate new ones, as in GReaT~\cite{borisov2023great}, REaLTabFormer~\cite{solatorio2023realtabformer} and TabLoft~\cite{chen2026tabloft}. \emph{Prompted LLMs} place a few real records in the prompt of a pre-trained LLM and ask it for new ones, without any training, as in CLLM~\cite{seedat2024cllm} and EPIC~\cite{kim2024epic}.
+Tabular SDG methods fall into four families that differ in how they produce records. \emph{Interpolation and statistical methods} derive new records directly from the real ones or from a simple probabilistic model of them: SMOTE places a new minority-class record on the line segment between two neighbouring records of that class~\cite{chawla2002smote}, and Bayesian networks estimate the dependencies between features and sample new records from them~\cite{zhang2014privbayes,cai2021privmrf}. \emph{Deep generative models} train a neural network to approximate the joint distribution of the features and sample from it: CTGAN trains a generator against a discriminator, TVAE learns a compressed representation of the records~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, and diffusion models such as TabDDPM, CoDi and TabSyn learn to reverse a gradual corruption of the records~\cite{kotelnikov2023tabddpm,lee2023codi,zhang2024tabsyn}; relational and cross-silo variants extend these models to several tables or institutions~\cite{fan2020relational,shankar2024silofuse}. \emph{Fine-tuned language models} write each record as a sentence, fine-tune a language model on these sentences and generate new ones, as in GReaT~\cite{borisov2023great}, REaLTabFormer~\cite{solatorio2023realtabformer} and TabLoft~\cite{chen2026tabloft}. \emph{Prompted LLMs} place a few real records in the prompt of a pre-trained LLM and ask it for new ones, without training, as in CLLM~\cite{seedat2024cllm} and EPIC~\cite{kim2024epic}.
 
 \subsection{Synthetic Data Generation in Small Data}
-Most of the previously mentioned generators were designed and benchmarked on datasets with tens or hundreds of thousands of records, such as Adult (48,842 records) or Covertype (581,012 records)~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, and their behaviour on a few hundred records has received far less attention. Table~\ref{tab:positioning} summarizes, for each family, how records are produced and its main limitation when data are small. Interpolation and statistical methods, as well as deep generators, are fitted only to the real records, so they cannot add information beyond what those records contain~\cite[Sec.~2.8]{cover2006elements}. They also treat every column as an anonymous number: they do not know that a column holds a patient's age or cholesterol level, and therefore cannot use what is known about these variables. Deep generators and fine-tuned language models have many parameters by design, which a few hundred records cannot estimate reliably, so they may produce records outside the observed ranges and types, or reproduce training records~\cite{hyeong2022tabularmia}. Prompted LLMs need no training and bring domain knowledge, but nothing ensures that their output contains the configured number of records with the correct columns and types, and the cost of obtaining it is not reported; CLLM~\cite{seedat2024cllm} filters the generated records by their learning dynamics and EPIC~\cite{kim2024epic} designs balanced prompts, but both concern the quality of the records that are kept rather than the size, structure and cost of the output. As discussed in Section~\ref{sec:intro}, all families are also usually compared on a single run. \system keeps the advantages of prompted LLMs and adds the control layer that they lack, and its evaluation repeats every experiment to measure the variation between runs.
