 \section{Discussion and Limitations}
 \label{sec:discussion}
 %======================================================================
-The objective of this work was to determine whether \system makes LLM-based SDG a reliable and useful option for small healthcare datasets. Reliability is achieved, and it is achieved by the control layer: the same LLM, prompted directly, never delivered the configured dataset, whereas \system completed it, recorded its cost and caught the failures of a less disciplined model at almost no extra cost (RQ1). Usefulness is real but bounded and depends on the LLM: the synthetic records preserve the distribution of the real data without revealing real records (RQ2), they help the classifiers that fail on the minority class (RQ3), and with a strong LLM they give the largest median gain among the generators compared, while a weak LLM can lose as much as direct prompting does (RQ4). The alternatives trade one property for another: curation is more stable but discards records, and tree-based generators fit the real distributions more closely by staying close to the real records. \system is not the best choice in three situations. With a weak LLM it does not improve on direct prompting in utility, and on kidney disease, with mixed types and the most missing values, GPT-4o mini lost 10.3 pp of F1 with or without the control layer, so a weak LLM should not be used for such data. When the goal is to reproduce the real distribution as closely as possible, tree-based and statistical generators are better, but they do so by staying close to the real records, which raises the membership-attack risk. And when stability matters more than gain, the CLLM filter changes the data least, at the price of an incomplete dataset; since the two address different failures, combining the control layer with such a filter is a natural extension. No generator, however, improves every dataset, which is consistent with the data-processing inequality~\cite[Sec.~2.8]{cover2006elements}: SDG cannot replace missing patients, and its benefit is bounded by the knowledge the generator brings. In practice, the choice of LLM matters most, temperature is the one parameter that changes fidelity, cost is better measured in tokens than in time (generation time varied by a factor of 1.7 between repeated runs), and the effect of synthetic records should be checked per classifier before they are used.
+The results come from the LLM and the control layer together: the LLM brings domain knowledge, and the control layer turns it into a dataset that satisfies its integrity constraints, which no LLM did alone, at almost no extra cost (RQ1). The usefulness is real but bounded (RQ2-RQ4). \system is not the best choice in three situations. With a weak LLM it does not improve utility over direct prompting, and on kidney disease GPT-4o mini lost 10.3 pp of F1 with or without it. When the goal is the closest fit to the real distribution, tree-based and statistical generators are better, at a higher membership-attack risk. When stability matters more than gain, the CLLM filter changes the data least, at the price of missing records; combining it with the control layer is a natural extension. No generator improves every dataset: SDG cannot replace missing patients~\cite[Sec.~2.8]{cover2006elements}. In practice, the LLM matters most, and synthetic records should be checked per classifier.
 
-The conclusions are subject to several limitations. Ten datasets act as independent blocks, and six for the control-layer and LLM variants, which limits the power of the tests; the divisions are not stratified. The datasets are public and widely used, so they may be part of the LLMs' training data and part of the fidelity may reflect memorized records rather than generation; the membership-inference attack does not test for this, and an evaluation on private data is left for future work. The Parkinson's dataset contains several recordings per subject, so recordings of one person can appear in both sets. The columns and types are inferred from the full dataset rather than from the training set alone. The parameters in Table~\ref{tab:params} were fixed and not tuned; the sensitivity study varies three of them one at a time, not jointly. The temperature differs between providers, and part of the Claude models' higher fidelity may reflect it (Section~\ref{sec:rq1}). \system does not filter synthetic records that are close to real ones: privacy is measured with one distance-based attack, not enforced, and no result establishes differential privacy~\cite{dwork2014dp}. The \system runs on the six original datasets predate the counter of delivered records, so their size follows from the completion loop rather than from a logged count. The CLLM baseline reimplements its curation step on our direct-prompting output rather than running the original code. The corrections were exercised only by GPT-4.1 mini, and records obtained in the last correction round or in the completion loop are not validated again. Context selection is not class-stratified, so a rare class can be missing from a prompt. The LLMs come from two commercial providers and locally served models were not evaluated; one of them, Claude Haiku 5.5, declined to imitate patient records under the control layer's prompts, which shows that a provider's safety behaviour can block generation on healthcare data. The established generators use default settings, and TabDDPM and GReaT, which need a GPU, were not included.
+The study has several limitations. Ten datasets (six for the variants) limit the power of the tests, and the divisions are not stratified. The datasets are public, so part of the fidelity may reflect records memorized by the LLMs, which the attack does not test; private data are left for future work. Parkinson's recordings of one subject can fall in both sets. Columns and types are inferred from the full dataset. The parameters in Table~\ref{tab:params} were fixed and not tuned; the sensitivity study varies three of them one at a time, not jointly. The temperature differs between providers, and part of the Claude models' higher fidelity may reflect it (Section~\ref{sec:rq4}). Privacy is measured with one attack, not enforced, and no result establishes differential privacy~\cite{dwork2014dp}. Delivered counts were not logged for the GPT runs of \system on the six original datasets. The CLLM baseline reimplements its curation step rather than running the original code. The corrections were exercised only by GPT-4.1 mini, and records obtained in the last correction round or in the completion loop are not validated again. Context selection is not class-stratified, so a rare class can be missing from a prompt. Local LLMs were not evaluated, and Claude Haiku 5.5's refusals show that a provider's safety behaviour can block generation. The established generators use default settings, and GReaT, which needs a GPU, was not included.
 
-Synthetic healthcare records must not inform diagnosis or policy without domain review, and near-chance performance against one attack does not justify releasing sensitive data. The implementation, the experiment runner and the script that regenerates every table and figure are publicly available.\footnote{TODO(authors): add the public repository URL required by the ICDE artifact policy.}
