 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 %======================================================================
-Small healthcare datasets pose a dilemma for synthetic data generation: generators fitted to the available records can only recombine the patterns those records contain, whereas an LLM can add domain knowledge but returns text rather than a dataset. \system resolves the dilemma with a control layer that turns the responses of an LLM into complete synthetic datasets with the columns and types of the real data and a recorded cost, without training on the target data. On ten public healthcare datasets, direct prompting never delivered the configured dataset, with LLMs of two providers and even with responses forced into a fixed format, while \system did at 1.9\% more cost, and with the strongest LLM it gave the largest median F1 gain of fourteen generators. Because repeated runs of the same pipeline vary as much as most design choices, we recommend that SDG for small data be evaluated on repeated runs. Future work will evaluate locally served LLMs, class-aware context selection, privacy filters and the deployment across healthcare facilities outlined in Section~\ref{sec:deployment}.
+Generators fitted to small healthcare datasets can only recombine their patterns, whereas an LLM adds domain knowledge but returns text rather than a dataset. \system resolves this with a control layer that turns LLM responses into synthetic datasets without integrity violations, with a recorded cost, without training on the target data. On ten healthcare datasets, direct prompting violated integrity in every run, while \system never did, with fewer tokens per delivered record, and it achieved the largest median F1 gain of sixteen generators. Future work will evaluate local LLMs, class-aware context, privacy filters and the deployment of Section~\ref{sec:deployment}.
 
